@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmw:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/works/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia - Huna, taken down from the register
#
# Registered in traditions/registry-modern.ttl and moved here now that its one
# named work has been opened. Strand of the structure-driven entrance (SPEC
# section 14): the tradition stood on pm:corpusNamed with a documented contact
# route, and no question pointed at it.
#
# Why this one out of the register, and it is an examinability argument. The
# work is a modern author's report written to prove that a system works, and it
# says in so many words how it chose its cases: it prints the dreams that came
# true and omits the rest, and it has heard of no failure of the wind prayer.
# A transmission that states its own selection rule is worth more to the
# examination layer than one that hides it, and this one also names, at length,
# where a failure is to be put: in a complex of guilt in the low self of the
# operator or of the patient.
#
# Order of the file: the work; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# claim on which the whole construction rests, that two Hawaiian words name the
# selves of the living; fire; the force; the death prayer; the future; healing;
# the complex and where failure is put; the rebuilt future; weather; the daily
# practice. The dispute over the two words stands at the end of the file, and
# the Hawaiian side of it in traditions/hawaiian.ttl.
#
# On the identifiers. The work prints Hawaiian terms for some of what it claims
# (mana, aka, aumakua, unihipili, uhane, kala, anana). Where it does, the term
# is named in the definition; the identifiers are descriptive English, because
# the work's Hawaiian is itself what is disputed and an identifier built from it
# would assert the reading.
#

#
# The work
#

pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles"@en, "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles"@de ;
    skos:note "The edition as the title page in the full text gives it: The Secret Science Behind Miracles, by Max Freedom Long, author of Recovering the Ancient Magic, Kosmon Press, Los Angeles 6, California, 2208 West 11th St., with the year given in brackets as 1948. The text says in chapter XIX that it was written in 1947."@en ;
    skos:note "Access: the full text at archive.org under the identifier the-secret-science-behind-miracles-unveiling-the-huna-tradition-of-the-ancient-p, 590,627 bytes; the file name is that of the upload and not the identifier, and the request is redirected, so the regular route needs the metadata name and a client that follows the redirect. The text is a clean transcription, not an optical text layer, and carries no printed page numbers; places are cited by chapter and by the case numbers the work itself gives. The transcription prints chapter XV twice, word for word, and carries chapter XXI without its chapter heading, inside the text of chapter XX, under the title the contents give it. The DeVorss printing of 1954 under secretsciencebeh00long is access-restricted and answers 401 on the full text; it was not compared."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read at the wording: in chapter I the passage on the dictionary of 1865 and the root translation of unihipili and uhane; chapters II, III, IV, X, XI, XV, XVIII, XIX and XXII entire; and chapter XXI. Not read: the rest of chapter I, chapters V to IX, XII to XIV, XVI, XVII, XX and the appendix of Hawaiian words, about half the book; read only at single sentences found by term search are chapters V, VII, IX, XII, XVI and XVII, and no node stands on them."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Huna a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Huna"@en, "Huna"@de ;
    skos:definition "An early twentieth century system of three selves and directed vital force, presented as the secret teaching of Hawaiian priests."@en ;
    skos:note "Constructed by an outsider from theosophical and New Thought material and rejected by Hawaiian scholars as a representation of anything Hawaiian. Registered as the transmission it actually is, so that the ascription can be entered as false without the material vanishing from the record."@en ;
    skos:note "Moved from traditions/registry-modern.ttl on the first places read. The three decisions SPEC section 14 requires before the first node follow."@en ;
    skos:note "One: the contact route stays documented, and the work documents it itself, which is unusual. It names the sources its system was put together from or checked against: William Tufts Brigham as teacher and informant; J. W. Dunne for the dream method; Nandor Fodor's Encyclopaedia of Psychic Science as the source of most cases; Dresser's Quimby Manuscripts for Quimby; Baron Eugene Ferson's classes in Honolulu for the accumulation of force; Freud for the complex; Theosophy, Christian Science, New Thought, Unity and the Oahspe Bible as neighbours it measures itself against. The holding carries pmt:NewThought on the same Quimby volume, so what this work says of Quimby is reception and is not entered here."@en ;
    skos:note "Two: the edition is the Kosmon Press printing named on the work node. The work is written in English by its author and has no original in another language, so the requirement of two translations does not apply and is not claimed as met. The Hawaiian words it builds on are a different matter, and the two sources read against them, a dictionary of 1865 and Malo's account in Emerson's translation, are named at the dispute at the end of this file."@en ;
    skos:note "Three: the number of citable places. Ninety-one nodes below, all from the chapters read at the wording, and the dispute at the end."@en ;
    skos:note "What kind of witness this is, so that no node is read as more than it is. The work is a single modern author's report. Almost every case in it reaches the reader through that author: some are his own experience, some are the first-person account of his teacher Brigham as he says he took it down soon afterwards in Brigham's words as nearly as he could, and some are told to him by third persons. Nothing in it is a text of the Hawaiian priests it ascribes the system to; it says itself that their language was never written until modern times and that the secret was handed down only from father to son. The attestation mode of each node says which of the three it stands on."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter I, the passage on the dictionary of 1865" .

#
# The claim the construction rests on
#

pmc:HunaUnihipiliIsTheLowSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Unihipili and uhane name the subconscious and the conscious spirit of a living man, found by translating the roots"@en, "Unihipili und uhane benennen den unterbewussten und den bewussten Geist eines lebenden Menschen, gefunden durch Übersetzung der Wurzeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author reasons that the priests must have had names for the elements of their magic, that the missionaries who made the dictionary did not know the magic and so translated those names wrongly, and that a translation of the short roots of which Hawaiian words are built will give the original meaning. He takes the two words for the two souls everyone in Hawaii agreed the priests had taught, unihipili and uhane, and reads their roots: u, to grieve; hane, to talk; uhi, to cover; pili, to adhere and serve; nihi, to do things secretly and carefully and to fear offending the gods. From these he concludes that unihipili is the subconscious, an animal-like spirit that remembers, handles the vital force and refuses to obey when it fears the gods, and uhane the conscious, talking spirit. In chapter IV the pair is stated as doctrine: the subconscious (unihipili) can remember but has only the reasoning of a dog or horse, the conscious (uhane) cannot remember but has inductive reason and hypnotic will."@en ;
    skos:note "The work prints, immediately before the root translation, the dictionary entry it overrides, and the entry says something else: the leg and arm bones of a person; one name of the class of gods called akuanoho, aumakua being another; the departed spirits of deceased persons. The author explains the difference by saying the missionaries had been given conflicting information. The dispute is entered at pmc:WhetherUnihipiliNamesASelfOfTheLiving."@en ;
    skos:note "The method is the thing to keep. A root reading of this kind can find in any word whatever its reader is looking for, and the author says he was looking for three things his teacher had told him to watch for, a consciousness, a force and a substance, and that he found the first two before the year was out and the third six years later. That is a description of a search that ends when it has found what it was told to find."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter I, the passage on the dictionary of 1865, and chapter IV, the opening section" .

pmc:HunaAumakuaIsTheHighSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Aumakua names a third, superconscious self of each person, a parental pair to which all prayer goes"@en, "Aumakua benennt ein drittes, überbewusstes Selbst jedes Menschen, ein Elternpaar, an das alles Gebet geht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Above man the priests attended only to the level directly above, the superconscious part of mind, called by several names of which the favourite was Aumakua, translated older, parental, utterly trustworthy spirit. As it takes two to be a parent it was a male and female pair; all prayers and rites were addressed to it, it was loved and not worshipped, received no sacrifice and laid no command. It is the consciousness that gives fire-immunity, works with the vital force mana and acts through the shadowy substance aka."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the opening section" .

#
# Fire
#

pmp:HunaFireWalkUnderProtection a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The walk over the cooling lava flow under the protection of three priests"@en, "Der Gang über den erkaltenden Lavastrom unter dem Schutz dreier Priester"@de ;
    skos:definition "Brigham's account of walking a lava overflow on Hawaii with three priests who had promised to let him walk under their protection. When rocks thrown on the surface showed it would bear their weight, the priests tied about three ti leaves to each bare foot, chanted in an archaic Hawaiian with mentions of legendary history and praise of the gods, and one of them beat the shimmering surface with a bunch of ti leaves; the oldest crossed first, about a hundred and fifty feet, and Brigham, who had refused to take off his boots, was shoved after him. The priests told him he must take off boots and socks because the goddess Pele had not agreed to keep boots from burning."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter II, case 1" .

pmc:HunaFireImmunity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The feet do not burn on red-hot lava, and what was not covered burns"@en, "Die Füße verbrennen nicht auf rotglühender Lava, und was nicht gedeckt war, verbrennt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the work calls fire-immunity through magic: in Brigham's walk the soles of his boots curled, burned and came off, his socks burned at the edges, and there was not a blister on his feet, not even where he had beaten out the fire in the socks; none of the priests had a blister though their ti leaves had long burned away. He felt intense heat on face and body and almost none in his feet. The protection is said to cover the walker's flesh and not his leather, which is the reason the priests gave for the boots."@en ;
    skos:note "Brigham's own control as the work reports it: twice afterwards at Kilauea he touched cooler lava with a finger, and with a stick, to test the theory of an insulating crust, and burned himself both times. The work treats this as excluding the physical explanation; it is a test of the crust and not of the walk."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter II, case 1 with its comment" .

pmc:HunaFireWalkYieldsImmunity a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The walk under protection keeps the feet from burning"@en, "Der Gang unter Schutz bewahrt die Füße vor dem Verbrennen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaFireWalkUnderProtection ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaFireImmunity ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "Brigham's account in the first person, as the author says he recorded it in his notes shortly after hearing it and reproduced in Brigham's own words and expressions as nearly as he could. No date of the walk is given. It is the only case of the chapter that a participant reports of his own body; the Tahitian, Burmese and Igorot cases of the same chapter are observers' reports and stand at the nodes below or are not entered."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter II, case 1" .

pmc:HunaGlanceAsideBlisters a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The one foot lifted when attention broke was the one that blistered"@en, "Der eine Fuß, der gehoben wurde, als die Aufmerksamkeit riss, war der mit der Blase"@de ;
    skos:definition "From a lecture by John G. Hill on a Tahitian fire walk, as the work reports it: a young white man, said to be something of a mystic, who held that his God would protect him as the brown men's magic protected them, crossed the stones barefoot behind the magician with a set face, as if holding his faith ready; a dog-fight broke out beside the pit, he glanced aside and lifted one foot suddenly, then set his face again and went on. That foot was found to have a large blister on the sole."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure with the failure attached: a break in attention during the walk, and a burn at the one place in time it broke. The lecturer is said to have vouched for the data and made no comment on it; the reading is the author's, who sets it beside the head magician's invitation to guests to cross under his protection and the white guest whose feet and shoes were unharmed and whose face was burned."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaFireWalkUnderProtection ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter II, case 3" .

pmc:HunaTheBurnIsReadAsGuilt a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The walker who burns is judged guilty or not pure enough"@en, "Wer verbrennt, gilt als schuldig oder nicht rein genug"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two cases of the chapter in which the burn is a verdict on the walker. In Tahiti a native forced to walk the hot stones as an ordeal of guilt or innocence was badly burned, and the people decided he was guilty despite his denials and had not merited the protection of the goddess of the skies. At a temple of Agni in Burma, as the Englishman who filmed it told the author, the priests said their god kept the feet of the pure and holy from burning and those not pure enough got burned; he counted forty-three candidates and said only three got burned; one feeble old man fell and was dead before the attendants dragged him out. The author comments that some were not yet good magicians and announces a point on the nature of purification from sin in relation to the ability to perform fire-magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Where the failure is put. In both cases the outcome is read back into the walker, so that a burn does not count against the protection but for the guilt. The work's own frame is the same and is stated later as doctrine: what blocks the higher magic is a complex of guilt in the low self (pmc:HunaNoComplexBeforeHealing). The next case the chapter gives, Igorot descendants of head-hunters walking safely, is introduced by the author as showing that killing is no bar, which he does not reconcile with the purity rule here."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaFireWalkUnderProtection ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter II, cases 3 and 4 with the comment on case 4" .

#
# The force
#

pmp:HunaChargingTheThrowingStick a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A heavy stick charged with bodily force by an effort of mind and thrown at the enemy"@en, "Ein schwerer Stab, durch eine Anstrengung des Geistes mit Körperkraft geladen und auf den Feind geworfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The old practice of holding heavy wooden sticks in the hands and by an effort of mind causing bodily electricity to enter and charge the stick heavily. In battle the priests stood in the rear lines, charged large sticks and threw them at one of the enemy."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaChargedStickStuns a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The charged stick knocks even the strongest warrior unconscious"@en, "Der geladene Stab schlägt selbst den stärksten Krieger bewusstlos"@de ;
    skos:definition "On contact with the charged sticks even the strongest warriors were often made unconscious. Brigham is said to have tested such sticks and found them capable of what seemed an electric shock of a peculiar kind, which numbed the limb touched and made the head swim."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaChargedStickYieldsStunning a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The charged stick stuns"@en, "Der geladene Stab betäubt"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaChargingTheThrowingStick ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaChargedStickStuns ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "Ascribed to the priests of former times by the author, with Brigham's test of the sticks reported in one sentence and without its conditions. The author adds in his own voice that an element of hypnotic suggestion must not be overlooked in such performances."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmp:HunaSurchargeByMentalCommand a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A surcharge of force accumulated by a mental command and drawn out of another by the hands"@en, "Eine Überladung an Kraft, durch einen geistigen Befehl gesammelt und mit den Händen aus einem anderen gezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ferson's method as taught in his Honolulu classes and adopted by the work: the pupil gives himself the mental command to accumulate a surcharge of force; when satisfied it is there, he places his hands on the shoulders of an uncharged pupil and draws them slowly away. In the work's final chapter the same method is recommended for building up an increased charge to be laid on another with thoughts of healing."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7, and chapter XXII" .

pmc:HunaOneSidedPull a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The charged body pulls the less charged one and feels no pull itself"@en, "Der geladene Körper zieht den weniger geladenen und spürt selbst keinen Zug"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the surcharge was sufficient, the uncharged pupil was pulled strongly after the withdrawn hands and the charged one felt no pull. The author saw Ferson charge a folding chair so that a sensitive young woman walking past was almost violently pulled down onto it, and pull the same woman towards a ten-inch cement wall from its other side so that two men had to hold her, while he stood erect and did not sway. The rule the work draws: the object with the heavier charge pulls the less charged to it, regardless of size or weight, and feels no reaction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaSurchargeYieldsPull a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The accumulated surcharge pulls another body"@en, "Die gesammelte Überladung zieht einen anderen Körper"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaSurchargeByMentalCommand ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaOneSidedPull ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "The author reports the chair and the wall as things he saw, and the dog as a thing he did; the subjects in the first two were Ferson's pupils, the one he calls the most sensitive in the class among them. The class itself, the work says, discussed whether suggestion played a part, which is what led to the test on the dog (pmc:HunaTheDogTestAgainstSuggestion)."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaTheDogTestAgainstSuggestion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The pull tried on a dog, because dogs are not known to be suggestible"@en, "Der Zug an einem Hund erprobt, weil Hunde nicht als suggestibel gelten"@de ;
    skos:definition "To test the pull without the possible implication of suggestion, the author and the owner of a small bull terrier went through the exercise of accumulating force and placed their charged hands on the rump of the dog, which was made to stand with its head pointed away. Both are said to have drawn the dog backwards several inches despite its clawing at the rug, and to have felt no pull on their own hands or bodies."@en ;
    skos:note "A control the practitioners built themselves, against the one alternative they named, and reported with its result. What it does not exclude is the hand: the charged hands rested on the dog's rump and were withdrawn, and the measure was how far the dog moved backwards after them. No count of trials is given, and no trial in which the dog did not move."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmp:HunaLayingOnHandsWithCommand a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Hands laid on the sick with a willed and spoken command that the force enter and strengthen them"@en, "Hände auf den Kranken gelegt mit dem gewollten und gesprochenen Befehl, die Kraft möge eintreten und ihn stärken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Laying the hands on the sick and making a willed command that one's force enter them and strengthen them; the will accompanied by the spoken word forms a suggestion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaAnyHealthyPersonCanHelpTheSick a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Almost any healthy person can help the sick in this way"@en, "Fast jeder gesunde Mensch kann so dem Kranken helfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's conviction from his own observation and study of natural healers: almost any healthy person can help the sick by laying hands on them with the willed command, and the suggestion so formed may be highly effective."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaLayingOnHandsYieldsHelp a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The hands with the command help the sick"@en, "Die Hände mit dem Befehl helfen dem Kranken"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaLayingOnHandsWithCommand ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaAnyHealthyPersonCanHelpTheSick ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    skos:note "No case is given at the place; the sentence is a generalisation from observation in the author's voice. Note what it is not: it is not ascribed to the priests, and the word may in the sentence leaves the outcome open."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaWillBreathingRunners a pm:Attributing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Messengers whose speed and endurance came from will and breathing"@en, "Läufer, deren Schnelligkeit und Ausdauer aus Willen und Atem kamen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In Hawaii, as in Tibet according to a recent book, a combination of will and breathing was used to gain magical aid in running long distances: specially trained messengers of the high chiefs, whose speed and endurance surpassed by far that of men not able to use this magic."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:HunaRunnersSpeedAndEndurance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaRunnersSpeedAndEndurance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Speed and endurance in running long distances beyond what others reach"@en, "Schnelligkeit und Ausdauer im Langstreckenlauf über das von anderen Erreichte hinaus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity ascribed to the trained messengers: speed and endurance far surpassing men without the magic."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmp:HunaBerberStoneInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A hen's blood on the face of a stone in a pit, and an invocation to the spirit in it"@en, "Hühnerblut auf der Fläche eines Steins in einer Grube, und eine Anrufung an den Geist darin"@de ;
    skos:definition "W. R. Stewart's account, as the work reports it, of a night rite shown him by his Berber teacher Lucchi, whom the work counts a kahuna: steps cut into a hillside under doors like cellar doors led to a rock; by torchlight a hen was killed, its blood let fall on the face of the stone and an invocation spoken to the spirit supposed to reside in it; the hen dropped before the stone rose in the air and pressed against it, and Stewart, stepping close with his torch, felt a powerful magnetic pull that almost jerked him against the rock."@en ;
    skos:note "The work counts the Berber teacher of Stewart among the kahunas, which is its own thesis that the same secret was kept in Polynesia and North Africa, stated in chapter I. The node records the rite as the work carries it and asserts nothing about Berber practice."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

pmc:HunaStandBackUntilTheHenIsAbsorbed a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Every dealing with such spirits follows the ritual exactly; he should have stood back until the hen was spent"@en, "Jeder Umgang mit solchen Geistern folgt genau dem Ritual; er hätte zurückbleiben sollen, bis die Henne aufgezehrt war"@de ;
    skos:definition "Lucchi stated that all dealings with such spirits must follow a carefully observed ritual and that any change in it might cause trouble. Stewart had changed the course of the rite by stepping close to the rock at the wrong time; he should have stood back until all the life force in the hen had been absorbed by the spirit, which needed it to comply with the requests of the invocation, after which the hen would have dropped. Lucchi pulled him back and insisted that they leave."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaBerberStoneInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter III, the comment on case 7" .

#
# The death prayer
#

pmp:HunaDeathPrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The death prayer: enslaved ghost spirits charged with force from food and sent along the scent of the victim"@en, "Das Todesgebet: versklavte Geister, aus Speise mit Kraft geladen und der Witterung des Opfers nachgeschickt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's account of the death prayer, which it names anana. To use it the priest had to inherit from another priest one or more, usually about three, ghostly subconscious spirits, or capture and enslave them by suggestion. He called them and gave them orders to absorb force from food and drink placed on a mat surrounded with ceremonial objects, small white stones and pieces of wood, called the papa; they were to catch the scent from a bit of hair or soiled garment of the victim, follow it as a dog follows a track, and enter the victim's body by means of the paralysing shock of the surcharge given them. The order is quoted from one recorded case: O Lono, listen to my voice; this is the plan: rush upon ... and enter; enter and curl up; curl up and straighten out."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8, preliminary notes" .

pmc:HunaDeathByPrayer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A numbness rising from the feet over three days to the heart, and death"@en, "Eine Taubheit, die von den Füßen in drei Tagen bis zum Herzen steigt, und der Tod"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the death prayer is said to do: the spirits take the vital force of the victim and store it in their ghostly bodies; as it is withdrawn from the feet a numbness comes that rises over three days to knees, hips and at last the solar plexus or heart, at which the victim dies. The spirits then return to their master, who has them play until they use up the force, which is what one would call poltergeist activity."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8, preliminary notes" .

pmc:HunaDeathPrayerYieldsDeath a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The death prayer kills"@en, "Das Todesgebet tötet"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaDeathPrayer ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaDeathByPrayer ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "The ground the work gives is a count it does not print: over several years, the author says, he checked through doctors frequenting the Queen's Hospital in Honolulu, and not a year passed but one or more victims died despite all the hospital could do, the old doctors recognising the familiar symptoms year after year. No year, no number and no name is given, and the symptom by which the cases were recognised is the one the doctrine predicts, so that the count cannot separate a death prayer from any other ascending paralysis the doctors chose to call by that name."@en ;
    skos:note "The work also states that none of the usual explanations, a poison or dying of fear, are true, because almost never did the victim know he was being prayed against. Both of its cases are offered as proof of this, and in the second the victim, asked by Brigham whether he thought he was being prayed to death, is said to have been instantly frightened within an inch of his life."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8, preliminary notes" .

pmc:HunaDeathPrayerNeedsGuilt a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The death prayer takes only a victim with a deep sense of guilt for wrongs done to others"@en, "Das Todesgebet trifft nur ein Opfer mit tiefem Schuldgefühl für Unrecht an anderen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The killing of a person by magic was thought to depend on whether the victim had a deep sense of guilt caused by wrongs done others. Such a guilt sense made the attack of the subconscious spirits successful; without it the victim's subconscious would ward them off. In chapter XV the same is said with a weaker word: the shock of the charge breaks down the victim's resistance, particularly if it harbours a guilt thought or complex."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HunaDeathByPrayer ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HunaVictimsSenseOfGuilt ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the sense of guilt the subconscious of the victim would successfully ward off the attacking spirits." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    skos:note "The condition is inside the victim, and the work never says how it would be known before the prayer is sent. It is used afterwards in both directions: Brigham turns the prayer back on the ground that the boy was guilty of no actual sin, and in the case of the Irishman the grandmother's grievance supplies the wrong. Chapter IV states it as necessary, chapter XV as the case in which it works best; the node is set at stated necessity on chapter IV, which names the consequence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, the comment after case 8, and chapter XV" .

pmc:HunaVictimsSenseOfGuilt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A deep sense of guilt for wrongs done others, held as a complex in the low self"@en, "Ein tiefes Schuldgefühl für an anderen begangenes Unrecht, als Komplex im niederen Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition in the intended victim on which the death prayer is said to depend: a deep sense of guilt, caused by wrongs done others, held as a complex."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, the comment after case 8" .

pmc:HunaReturnedSpiritsAttackTheSender a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Spirits sent back by another priest attack their master unless he has cleansed himself"@en, "Von einem anderen Priester zurückgeschickte Geister greifen ihren Herrn an, wenn er sich nicht gereinigt hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the victim was rescued by another priest and the spirits sent back with orders to attack their master, they might attack with fatal results. To avoid this danger the priest sending the spirits usually performed a ritual of cleansing, kala; or, most often, the person who had hired him and vouched that the victim deserved the punishment was named as the one to be attacked should the spirits be sent back before their task was done."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution with its remedy, and the remedy is to move the consequence of failure from the operator onto his client. That is the device pmc:ThePenaltyLaidOnAnotherParty describes, here written into the commission of the operation in advance."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaDeathPrayer ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8, preliminary notes" .

pmp:HunaTurningBackTheDeathPrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Turning the death prayer back: the spirits argued over to the victim's side and commanded home three times"@en, "Das Todesgebet zurückwenden: die Geister auf die Seite des Opfers überredet und dreimal heimbefohlen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Brigham's own account of turning back a death prayer sent against one of his men on Mauna Loa. The trick of it, he says, is to put up an argument so cunning that the spirits think their master must be a devil to send them against one so pure; he praised them, told them how they had been captured and imposed upon and kept from the heaven that awaited them, how innocent the boy was and how vile the priest, and when he judged them ready to revolt he roared the command to return and visit the priest with ten times the punishment, three times, and then held his will fixed on it. The ti leaves usually brushed over the victim he did without."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8 (B)" .

pmc:HunaTheBoyRestoredAndThePriestDead a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Within an hour the boy's legs were good, and the priest who sent the prayer was dead by morning"@en, "Binnen einer Stunde waren die Beine des Jungen gut, und der Priester, der das Gebet gesandt hatte, war am Morgen tot"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the longest hour in history Brigham felt the tension in the air go in a flash, and the boy whispered legs good; he massaged the limbs until the toes moved, and in an hour the boy was up and eating. Going down to the boy's village later he was told that on that night the old priest, who had forbidden his people dealings with white men on pain of the death prayer, had woken screaming, fanned himself with ti leaves against his spirits, said that he had neglected to cleanse himself and the white priest had taken advantage of him, fallen frothing and died by morning."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8 (B)" .

pmc:HunaTurningBackYieldsRestoration a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer turned back restores the victim and kills the sender"@en, "Das zurückgewandte Gebet stellt das Opfer wieder her und tötet den Sender"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaTurningBackTheDeathPrayer ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaTheBoyRestoredAndThePriestDead ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "Brigham's first-person account as the author says he transcribed it from his notes shortly after an evening with him, in his words as nearly as possible. The second half, the priest's death, is what the villagers told the boy and the boy told Brigham; no one in the account saw it except the villagers. Brigham closes by saying he could never understand why a priest who had found out by psychic means that the boy was hired had not found out that he was being turned on, and accounts for it by supposing the priest went to sleep at dusk."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8 (B)" .

pmc:HunaDeathPrayerLiftedOnTerms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The rising numbness stopped the same night the terms were accepted"@en, "Die steigende Taubheit hörte in derselben Nacht auf, in der die Bedingungen angenommen waren"@de ;
    skos:definition "A young Irish taxi driver in Honolulu who had drawn a Hawaiian girl away from her betrothed felt his feet go to sleep; the prickling numbness rose in fifty hours to his waist, and no cause was found in hospital. An old doctor recognised the symptoms of the death prayer, learned of the girl's grandmother and her veiled threats, and asked her as a friend whether anything could be done; she said she was no kahuna and knew nothing of it, but that if the man would take the next ship for America and never return or write he might recover. The man was terrified once it was explained to him and agreed in the early afternoon; that night he was on his feet and caught a ship."@en ;
    skos:note "Offered by the work as a case in which fear could play no part, because the man feared nothing and thought the threats piffle. The account itself puts the recovery after he was made to understand and became terrified, which is the one moment in it at which fear did enter. The grandmother denies being a priest and the work does not say who sent the prayer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter IV, case 8 (A)" .

#
# The future
#

pmp:HunaDreamRecord a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Pencil and paper taken to bed, and every dream written down on waking"@en, "Bleistift und Papier mit ins Bett genommen, und jeder Traum beim Erwachen aufgeschrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dunne's method, taken over by the work: the experimenter takes pencil and paper to bed and impresses on himself the determination to write down his dreams whenever he wakes in the night and can recall them, in order to watch for those that relate not to the past or present but to the future. The author's additions from his own experience: provide a weak night light that does not hurt the eyes, tell the sleep self what a pleasure it is to wake and write down dreams, and ideally speak them into a recording instrument."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 16 with its comment" .

pmc:HunaDreamingIntoTheFuture a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Dreaming events that come about days later, in detail beyond the chance of imagining them"@en, "Ereignisse träumen, die Tage später eintreten, in Einzelheiten jenseits dessen, was sich ausdenken ließe"@de ;
    skos:definition "In 1926 in Honolulu, on the night after reading Dunne, the author dreamed of a strange fat man asking his help with an optical invention, of a black enamelled lid with a square hole and an hourglass-shaped setscrew, of a latticed kitchen with a tall slim man and a small Hawaiian woman, and of developing sensitive paper in three strange white trays to show a scale and a pointer, and saying, well, it works. On the Thursday the fat man came into his camera store wanting a ray of light split to record a weight scale; the lid matched; the kitchen was the mechanic's, the woman his wife, the trays a Japanese kind he had acquired in a trade the day before; and the test came out as dreamed. The work states the capacity generally: most people dream of future events and do not remember the dreams."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 16" .

pmc:HunaDreamRecordYieldsForesight a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The dream record shows the future"@en, "Das Traumprotokoll zeigt die Zukunft"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaDreamRecord ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaDreamingIntoTheFuture ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "The author's own night, with the notes quoted. He says by the time of the test he had told of his dreams and exhibited the notes, so the closing words were spoken by people who knew them; and he marks one part of the dream as wrong, the lid never brought to his desk. How the notes were chosen is stated at the place and entered separately (pmc:HunaOmitsTheDreamsThatFailed)."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 16" .

pmc:HunaOmitsTheDreamsThatFailed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "I omit all dreams which did not come true"@en, "Ich lasse alle Träume aus, die nicht eintrafen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The report of the author's first night of the dream record is introduced with the words: my notes ran like this, and in parentheses, I omit all dreams which did not come true. The several dreams of the restless night are thereby reduced in print to the one sequence that came true, together with the one part of it that did not."@en ;
    skos:note "The selection rule of a report, stated by its author at the place where it acts. The holding has many reports that select their cases and almost none that say so; this one says so and then offers the remainder as proof. The number of dreams omitted is not given, so the hit cannot be set against a denominator, and the reader is told only that there was one."@en ;
    skos:note "The same rule stands at the weather case of chapter XXI in another form (pmc:HunaHeardOfNoFailure), and at the crystal-gazing case in its opposite form, where the author does say that in other sittings no vision of the future appeared (pmc:HunaCrystalYieldsForesight). A count of the three gives the work's habit: two places where the failures are kept out by statement, one where they are mentioned and not counted."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 16" .

pmc:HunaGrumblingStopsTheDreams a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Grumbling at waking to write taught the low self the practice was unwanted, and the dreams of the future stopped"@en, "Das Murren beim Aufwachen zum Schreiben lehrte das niedere Selbst, die Übung sei unerwünscht, und die Zukunftsträume hörten auf"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author tired of the experiment, like most others, and made the mistake of grumbling to himself when sleepily struggling to write down a dream; the light hurt his eyes, and his low self got the idea that the whole matter was undesirable. It formed a complexed opinion of it and thereafter seemed to refuse to pass on to him any picture of a future event it obtained. Hence the advice of the weak night light and of telling the sleep self what a pleasure it is."@en ;
    skos:note "The failure is reported, and it is put in the operator's low self: the dreams of the future stopped because a part of him refused to deliver them, not because there were none to deliver. The work names the reason most people give up the method in the same breath: the dreams show ordinary daily events hit and miss, and an unbroken night is preferable."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaDreamRecord ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16" .

pmc:HunaInwardSenseWithinAMonth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Within a month one learns to know by an inward sense which dreams belong to the future"@en, "Binnen eines Monats lernt man an einem inneren Sinn erkennen, welche Träume der Zukunft angehören"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the author's experience is a criterion, one will learn inside a month to know by some inward sense whether a dream belongs to the future and is worth recording or is a common dream of little significance. His father, he says, recognised such dreams by an inner sensing and asked the family at breakfast to remember them and help him check later."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success announced in advance, counted apart from the conditions of failure as SPEC section 15 requires. It is reported only by the dreamer, and it is offered as a filter that decides what is recorded, so that a dream it rejects is not written down and cannot later be counted as a miss."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16 and case 17" .

pmp:HunaSuggestionDuringSleep a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A recorded suggestion played at low volume during sleep, night after night"@en, "Eine aufgezeichnete Suggestion, im Schlaf leise abgespielt, Nacht für Nacht"@de ;
    skos:definition "A carefully prepared record, started during the night by a clock and made to repeat or play through, through an under-the-pillow speaker at low volume; in a few nights it ceases to wake the sleeper. The low self, sensitive to sound during sleep, hears the suggestion and makes thought forms of the words, which lodge in its shadowy body untouched by waking rationalisation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16" .

pmc:HunaSleepSuggestionAccepted a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The low self accepts the night suggestion and reacts to it more fully with each repetition"@en, "Das niedere Selbst nimmt die nächtliche Suggestion an und reagiert mit jeder Wiederholung stärker"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the night suggestion is said to achieve: the low self freely accepts the suggestions and reacts to them more and more fully as the nightly repetition goes on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16" .

pmc:HunaSleepSuggestionNeedsConfidentExpectancy a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The night suggestion takes only if the waking self has assured itself it is acceptable and workable"@en, "Die nächtliche Suggestion greift nur, wenn das wache Selbst sich versichert hat, dass sie annehmbar und wirksam ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rationalising by the middle self ties to the words of a suggestion a contradictory string of thought forms saying it is not workable. One using recorded suggestions in sleep will therefore do well to assure himself that what he has the machine read is acceptable and that the low self will respond and make it workable; this attitude of confident expectancy leaves the low self open to accept the suggestions."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HunaSleepSuggestionAccepted ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HunaConfidentExpectancy ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without it the middle self ties to the suggestion a contradictory string of thought forms saying that it is not workable." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16" .

pmc:HunaConfidentExpectancy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Confident expectancy in the waking self"@en, "Zuversichtliche Erwartung im wachen Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attitude of the middle self that the suggestions it has chosen are acceptable and will be made workable by the low self."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16" .

pmc:HunaPrevisionNotOnCommand a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A hypnotised subject foresees only by accident and never on the operator's command"@en, "Ein Hypnotisierter sieht nur zufällig voraus und nie auf Befehl des Operateurs"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hypnotised subjects have often been reported as telling of events before they occurred, but only as by accident; prevision cannot be had on the command of the operator. The work takes this as proof that the subconscious, which responds to suggestion, cannot see the future and must be shown it by a being of superior powers, the Aumakua. Spirits speaking through mediums likewise foretell correctly only as by accident and cannot do so at will."@en ;
    skos:note "A claim of absence stated as a test result: the capacity does not appear under one condition, command, and does appear without it. It is the one place in the chapter where the work names a condition under which its kind of foresight would be expected to fail, and it uses the failure as evidence for the doctrine."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, comment on case 16 and case 19" .

pmc:HunaIsolatedLowSelfExplainsFailedPredictions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The spirits that fail to foretell are low selves cut off from their middle self, guessing what the sitters expect"@en, "Die Geister, die falsch vorhersagen, sind von ihrem mittleren Selbst abgetrennte niedere Selbste, die raten, was die Sitzung erwartet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The records of psychical research hold instances of spirits foretelling correctly and instances of their failing dismally, the failure so frequent that it has brought spiritualism into question. The work's account of it: the low self of a dead person cut off from its middle self by some accident of dying loves to communicate and, lacking inductive reason, tries to answer any question, usually guessing or reading in the sitters' minds what they expect to hear; hence the seeming lies. When one learns to tell such isolated visitors from the normal spirit with both selves, one will not be deceived so often."@en ;
    skos:note "The frequent failure is acknowledged and assigned to a kind of speaker, so that the hits stand for the genuine spirit and the misses for the counterfeit one. Nothing in the passage says how the two are told apart before the prediction is checked."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 19, preliminary notes" .

pmp:HunaCrystalGazing a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Crystal gazing with a spoken request for vision of the future"@en, "Kristallschauen mit gesprochener Bitte um eine Schau der Zukunft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Crystal gazing produces a relaxation in which the low self enters a state like sleep while the middle self stands by and observes the dream-like images in the crystal. The scryer at Lovelock, Nevada, made a spoken request for vision of future events, addressed, the author says, without knowing to whom."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 18" .

pmc:HunaForeseeingOnRequest a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Two weeks of the enquirer's future seen in detail before he had chosen where to go"@en, "Zwei Wochen der Zukunft des Fragenden im Einzelnen gesehen, bevor er gewählt hatte, wohin er ging"@de ;
    skos:definition "In two sittings the scryer saw two parts of the author's future of about a week each, while he was travelling from town to town as a photographer and had not decided where to go: a small town on a railway line with a modern brick hotel, a red-haired young woman in white with a red-haired baby on a cottage porch, and Indians encamped near the hotel at a meeting; and a town along an old main road with a Globe Rooming House under a painted globe and a woman with dark hair and slightly crossed eyes. Within a month he got off the train at Mason, Nevada, met the woman and photographed the baby and the annual conclave of the tribes of the Carson Sink, and then went on to Yerrington, as the work spells it, and found the rooming house and the woman."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 18" .

pmc:HunaCrystalYieldsForesight a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The crystal shows the future on request"@en, "Der Kristall zeigt auf Bitte die Zukunft"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaCrystalGazing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaForeseeingOnRequest ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "The author is the enquirer and the one who checked the prediction, and he chose where to travel after hearing it; the second town he went to after Mason is the one in which he watched for the sign. He says in the comment that in other sittings no vision of the future appeared in answer to a request, and that at other times one came without request. Neither is counted."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter X, case 18 with its comment" .

#
# Healing
#

pmp:HunaHealingPrayerOverTheFracture a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The leg straightened, the hands pressed on the break, and a low chanted prayer for healing"@en, "Das Bein gerichtet, die Hände auf den Bruch gedrückt, und ein leise gesungenes Gebet um Heilung"@de ;
    skos:definition "At a beach party an elderly woman priest, grandmother-in-law of the author's friend J. A. K. Combs, knelt by a man who had broken his left leg above the ankle in a compound fracture, straightened the foot and leg, pressed on the place where the bone ends pushed out the skin, and began a low chanted prayer for healing; after a short silence her hands moved slightly and she took them away, saying quietly in Hawaiian, the healing is finished, stand up, you can walk."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, case 20" .

pmc:HunaInstantHealing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Instant healing: the broken or diseased part dissolved and reformed in the mould of the shadowy body"@en, "Sofortige Heilung: der gebrochene oder kranke Teil aufgelöst und in der Form des Schattenkörpers neu gebildet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the work calls instant healing through the High Self. The man rose, walked, and the leg showed no indication of the break. The explanation it gives: the High Self, using the high voltage of the force, dissolves the injured tissues into ectoplasm and resolidifies them in the unbroken mould of the shadowy body, which duplicates every cell; a cancer is likewise changed and remade into normal tissue."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, case 20 with its comment" .

pmc:HunaHealingPrayerYieldsInstantHealing a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer over the fracture heals it at once"@en, "Das Gebet über dem Bruch heilt ihn sofort"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaHealingPrayerOverTheFracture ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaInstantHealing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "Told to the author by Combs, who was present; the woman herself is not quoted beyond her closing words, and no name is given for her or the patient. The work says plainly that proof that the bone was dissolved and remade is difficult to give because nothing is to be seen, and turns to the apports of the séance room for it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, case 20" .

pmc:HunaNoComplexBeforeHealing a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Healing is granted only when no complexed doubt or unremoved conviction of sin or guilt remains"@en, "Heilung wird nur gewährt, wenn kein komplexhafter Zweifel und keine unbeseitigte Überzeugung von Sünde oder Schuld bleibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "There are certain conditions which must be made right, if they exist, before healing is granted: there must be no complexed doubt or conviction of sin or guilt that has not been cleared away. What has been called faith is a condition of freedom from any hindering complex. The complex, called by one priest the thing eating inside, is a belief held by the low self, correct or not, difficult to find once lodged and more difficult to remove."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HunaInstantHealing ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HunaFreedomFromHinderingComplex ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Healing is not granted while the complexed doubt or the conviction of sin or guilt remains." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    skos:note "The condition that runs through the whole work and decides where every failure goes. It lies in the low self of the patient or the operator, which the work says neither can easily inspect: we are unaware of such fixations and naturally conclude we have none. Its test is entered at pmc:HunaNoResultsMeansAComplex."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, comment on case 20" .

pmc:HunaFreedomFromHinderingComplex a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Faith as freedom from any hindering complex, the low self believing with the middle self"@en, "Glaube als Freiheit von jedem hinderlichen Komplex, das niedere Selbst glaubt mit dem mittleren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition healing presupposes. In chapter XVIII the same is restated: belief by the middle self is not faith; only when the low self also believes is there workable faith, and if the low self holds a fixed or slightly complexed belief contrary to that of the middle self it will refuse to obey."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, comment on case 20, and chapter XVIII" .

pmc:HunaKahunaSuccessAverage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The priests did not always succeed, but their average was so high there is little comparison with a million failures to one"@en, "Die Priester hatten nicht immer Erfolg, aber ihr Durchschnitt war so hoch, dass gegen eine Million Fehlschläge auf einen kaum ein Vergleich besteht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Praying Christians and mental healers sometimes get miraculous cures of body and purse, but not with certitude: there are a million failures to one successful demonstration. The priests did not always succeed in gaining the help of a High Self each time they asked, but their average of success was so high that there can be little comparison."@en ;
    skos:note "A comparative rate stated in the form of a count without any count behind it on either side, and the only sentence in the chapters read that concedes the priests failed at all. It is entered because it is the work's own measure of itself: the claim on which the system rests is a rate, and the work prints no rate."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, case 20, preliminary notes" .

pmp:HunaWashingAwayTheSins a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Ceremonial washing with the spoken suggestion that all sins are being washed away"@en, "Rituelle Waschung mit der gesprochenen Suggestion, alle Sünden würden fortgewaschen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The low self is best impressed by real and tangible things; the priests used water in ceremonial washing of the patient while giving the spoken suggestion that all sins were being washed away, and many other physical stimuli. All efforts to remove a complex are said to be far more successful if they combine a logical appeal to the patient's conscious self, mild suggestion, and a physical stimulus accompanying the suggestion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XI, comment on case 20" .

#
# The complex, and where failure is put
#

pmp:HunaShockSuggestionWithTheBucket a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The face thrust into a bucket of charged brackish water while the illness is commanded out"@en, "Das Gesicht in einen Eimer geladenen Brackwassers gedrückt, während der Krankheit zu weichen befohlen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's secret method of treating the complex: it is not necessary to search out the original complex; the low self of the patient is made to accept a suggestion contrary to its complex by an overpowering shock of a large charge of low force accompanying the suggestion. The case told to Brigham: a fisherman kept from crossing the beach to his canoe by a paralysis of the leg, then dizzy spells, then blindness; the priest worked long over a large wooden bucket of brackish water, stirring it with green ti leaves, adding grated yellow ginger and laying his palms on the surface, then had the patient thrust in his face, hold his breath and drink as much as he could, told that the water would drive out all that caused the trouble; with his hands on the patient's arms he commanded the illness to leave. The patient sagged over the bucket as if dazed and was left so for several minutes while the priest repeated that the ills had vanished never to return; then he was ordered across the beach."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV" .

pmc:HunaComplexReplaced a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The complex replaced by an implanted one: he crossed the beach, and the trouble did not return"@en, "Der Komplex durch einen eingepflanzten ersetzt: er überquerte den Strand, und das Leiden kehrte nicht wieder"@de ;
    skos:definition "He found that he could cross the beach without a return of the maladies, and the trouble did not return. The work states what happened in its own terms: the original complex was never located or drained off; it was replaced by another, a thought form implanted by the suggestion-shock."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV" .

pmc:HunaBucketYieldsReplacedComplex a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The shock with the suggestion replaces the complex"@en, "Der Schock mit der Suggestion ersetzt den Komplex"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaShockSuggestionWithTheBucket ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaComplexReplaced ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "Brigham did not see the final treatment; it was described to him at great length later, and he told it to the author, who says he understood it only years afterwards in the light of the secret. That the water carried a shocking charge is the author's reading of the account, stated as an undoubted fact. The earlier treatments are said to have cured each symptom only for the next to appear."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV" .

pmc:HunaDoNotThinkOfTheTrouble a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Told not to think of his trouble, the usual precaution against the return of the complex"@en, "Angewiesen, nicht an sein Leiden zu denken, die übliche Vorkehrung gegen die Wiederkehr des Komplexes"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the crossing the patient was told not to think of his trouble, a usual precaution taken by the priests to prevent the recurrence of the complex, and the work was done."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that moves a later failure in advance: if the trouble returns, the patient has thought of it. Chapter XVIII adds the same of the girl with the sore on her ankle, whose complex was dangerously easy to re-establish."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaShockSuggestionWithTheBucket ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV" .

pmp:HunaStrokingWithSuggestion a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The cheek stroked with the fingers and the beard told to stop growing"@en, "Die Wange mit den Fingern gestrichen und dem Bart gesagt, er höre auf zu wachsen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A Hawaiian woman at a dinner party offered a young white advertising salesman with fine skin to stop his beard growing, as she said many Hawaiian men had been relieved of shaving; he accepted in fun. She took him aside and stroked his right cheek with her fingers for a short time, saying that the beard would stop growing and he would be free of shaving. Found again on the author's advice, she reluctantly stroked the cheek once more and said the beard would return."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV, case 26" .

pmc:HunaBeardStoppedAndRestored a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A bare spot that grew from a dime to a half dollar, and the beard back in little over a week after the reversal"@en, "Eine kahle Stelle, die von Groschen- auf Halbdollargröße wuchs, und der Bart wieder da gut eine Woche nach der Umkehr"@de ;
    skos:definition "About two weeks after the stroking, which he had forgotten, the man noticed a place the size of a dime on his cheek where no beard grew; it grew larger day by day, and when it was as large as a half dollar he came to the author. In a little over a week after the second stroking the beard began to grow in the bare circle, and in a short time it was back to normal. The work draws from it that the low self can change bodily functions in response to suggestion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV, case 26" .

pmc:HunaStrokingYieldsBeardChange a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The stroking with the word stops the beard and the second stroking restores it"@en, "Das Streichen mit dem Wort hält den Bart an, und das zweite Streichen bringt ihn zurück"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaStrokingWithSuggestion ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaBeardStoppedAndRestored ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "The author saw the bare skin and the stubble returning, and gave the advice; the stroking itself he did not see. He calls it suggestion beyond doubt and says nothing of the woman's own account of what she did. The man did not believe at the first stroking, so the case is offered against the condition that the subject must believe, which the work does not draw out."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XV, case 26" .

pmc:HunaNoResultsMeansAComplex a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "If there are no results at all, hunt for a complex"@en, "Wenn es gar keine Ergebnisse gibt, such nach einem Komplex"@de ;
    skos:definition "It is very difficult for the individual to find out whether he has a fixed belief of a certain kind lodged in his low self; being unaware of such fixations we naturally conclude we have none. The test is best applied by watching the results we get after a period of faithful daily practice. If there are no results at all, hunt for a complex."@en ;
    skos:note "The work's only general instruction for what a failure means, and it reads the failure as the evidence for the condition rather than against the practice. The condition cannot be observed beforehand, by the work's own statement, and its presence is inferred from the very outcome it is meant to explain, so no run of the practice can count against it. That is the shape pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation describes, and this passage is entered there as a witness. What distinguishes it from most of that statement's witnesses is that it is written for the practitioner as a self-test: the person searching for the complex is the one who has just failed."@en ;
    skos:note "The neighbouring places agree. The dream record stops because the low self formed a complexed opinion (pmc:HunaGrumblingStopsTheDreams), the contact with the Something fails over a photograph whose offence no one could have named in advance (pmc:HunaThePictureBlocksTheContact), and the death prayer takes only a victim with guilt (pmc:HunaDeathPrayerNeedsGuilt). The final chapter gives the way out when the complex cannot be found: humour the low self and cease to do the things it considers sinful."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII" .

pmc:HunaTingleSignsTheContact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A tingling tells that the low self has obeyed and made the contact"@en, "Ein Kribbeln zeigt an, dass das niedere Selbst gehorcht und die Verbindung hergestellt hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "In telepathic exercise, whether with the High Self, another person or a departed spirit, flowing vital force causes a tingling sensation, a great help in determining whether the low self has obeyed and made contact. The contact the High Self makes of its own accord in sleep is often recognised by a tingling, usually in the region of the sacral plexus, at the moment of falling asleep or before; the author says he has often felt it on dozing off in the afternoon and woken rested immediately after. The elevator engineer of case 28 felt an electric tingling, sharp and short, at each contact and called it getting the ting-a-ling."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success announced in advance and reported only by the one who has it; counted apart from the conditions of failure. The author also feels it when talking about dead friends and takes it as their presence, so the same sign is read for two different contacts."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII" .

pmc:HunaBlockedPathMeansIllness a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A low self with a shame or guilt complex repels the High Self in sleep, and illness or death follows"@en, "Ein niederes Selbst mit Scham- oder Schuldkomplex weist das Hohe Selbst im Schlaf ab, und Krankheit oder Tod folgt"@de ;
    skos:definition "If our low self has a shame or guilt complex and repels the approach of the High Self during sleep, we face disaster: we become lost souls without higher guidance, lose our vitality and become ill; the path is blocked. The High Self seems able in due time to force a contact, since in sickness most people pray and open the door; but if the complex is too strong, the resulting illness or accident may end with death. The path must be kept open."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaReachingForTheSomething ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII" .

pmp:HunaReachingForTheSomething a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Day after day reaching out for the higher intelligence until the contact comes, then praying aloud and rising from the bed"@en, "Tag für Tag nach der höheren Intelligenz ausgreifen, bis die Verbindung kommt, dann laut beten und vom Bett aufstehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice of the elevator engineer of case 28, as he told it to the author: bedfast, out of work and funds, having tried Christian Science, New Thought and Unity without result, he concluded that all religious teachings were imperfect but that there must be a higher intelligence to be reached, and spent his time and strength day after day trying to reach out and find it. When the contact came, with the tingling, he cried out for help, declared he was leaving his bed to show his faith, and rose praying, step by step. Later he learned to make the contact almost at will and never let a morning or evening pass without it; he asked to be shown any danger ahead on the job and made the contact again on the spot to ask for guidance."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

pmc:HunaHealedOnRisingAndTheDaughterToo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Strength restored in minutes on rising, and the bedfast daughter pulled to her feet at the next contact"@en, "Kraft binnen Minuten beim Aufstehen wiederhergestellt, und die bettlägerige Tochter beim nächsten Kontakt auf die Füße gezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "He found himself growing stronger with each step and in a matter of minutes seemed completely cured. He could not make the contact again for several days; when it came he prayed for his daughter, bedfast for months, pulled her to her feet by the hands telling her to use her faith, and she too rose healed. At the next contact he prayed for his old job, went to the man who had dismissed him, said he was ready for work, and was handed the papers of an installation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

pmc:HunaReachingYieldsHealing a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The contact with the Something heals body and purse"@en, "Die Verbindung mit dem Etwas heilt Leib und Geldbeutel"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaReachingForTheSomething ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaHealedOnRisingAndTheDaughterToo ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "The engineer's story as he told it to the author; his name, the daughter's and the company's are not given. The case is chosen, the author says, from a number of cases of instant healing because it shows the flow of the force and because the people were of no particular religious belief."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

pmc:HunaDangerSense a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A sense of danger that grows and narrows to the place of the coming accident"@en, "Ein Gefahrensinn, der wächst und sich auf den Ort des kommenden Unfalls verengt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Warned by a strong sense of danger when an emergency was coming, the engineer found it growing until almost continuous and intensifying at one place as he went about the job; he stationed himself there with his foreman and others to watch, and accident after accident was caught in time. On the Honolulu hotel he told the author of a preliminary warning, traced it next morning to the top of a work elevator shaft on the roof, and with two men saw a workman with a barrow and a coil of stiff cable lift the gate as someone below started the elevator without the bell; the barrow tipped, the cable uncoiled and struck him from behind, and the three dragged him back from the shaft. For several years the company is said to have paid him a considerable yearly premium because no man was injured on any job he supervised."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

pmc:HunaContactYieldsDangerSense a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The daily contact gives warning of accidents"@en, "Der tägliche Kontakt warnt vor Unfällen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaReachingForTheSomething ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaDangerSense ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "The one case in the chapters read that the author arranged to check beforehand: he asked the engineer to tell him the details should a danger arise on the Honolulu job, was told of the preliminary warning, and afterwards had the same story from all those involved. What the check cannot show is the warnings that led to nothing, because a watched place where no accident came is a prevented accident in the engineer's account and the premium record counts the absence of injuries and not the warnings."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

pmc:HunaThePictureBlocksTheContact a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A photograph on the dresser stopped the contact for a day, and returning it restored it within an hour"@en, "Eine Fotografie auf der Kommode sperrte den Kontakt einen Tag lang, und ihre Rückgabe stellte ihn binnen einer Stunde wieder her"@de ;
    skos:definition "An Australian tile-layer on the building gave the engineer a modest nude study from his collection of fine photographs; he set it on the dresser in his hotel room in the evening and next morning could not make his usual contact, and tried all day without success. In the evening his eyes fell on the picture; he did not consider it wicked, but a suspicion grew that it had to do with the failure, and he returned it at once, telling the Australian why; an hour later he made the contact. Asked whether the picture was sinful, the Something gave no impression in reply. He told the author he never knew what it might consider a sin: he chewed tobacco and swore freely, but experience had taught him that certain little things might unexpectedly and inexplicably prevent the contact."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure with its failure attached and its cure tried, and the one in the work that comes nearest to an experiment: a single change, a day without contact, the change undone, the contact back. Two things keep it from being one. The condition was identified after the failure by looking for what had changed, so any change of that day would have served; and the practitioner says himself that what counts as such a condition cannot be known in advance. The author's reading, an old complex about sex or modesty roused by a physical stimulus, is added afterwards and is his."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaReachingForTheSomething ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

pmc:HunaBowToTheComplex a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "If one cannot get rid of such complexes, he must bow to them"@en, "Wer solche Komplexe nicht loswird, muss sich ihnen beugen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Over and over the point must be stressed: if one cannot get rid of such complexes, he must bow to them. The engineer had to give up his picture; the girl who danced and drank and had an open sore on her ankle could not get rid of her early belief that dancing and cocktails were sin, and would have been wise to give them up before the drastic operation became necessary. The final chapter repeats it: if you fail to gain liberation from false dogmas of sin, humour your complexed low self and cease to do the things it insists on considering sinful."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaReachingForTheSomething ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, comment on case 28, and chapter XXII" .

pmc:HunaPresenceSensedThroughTheDoor a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The test at the dark house: he knocked only when he sensed the author inside"@en, "Der Versuch am dunklen Haus: er klopfte nur, wenn er den Autor drinnen spürte"@de ;
    skos:definition "To test the engineer's claim that his trained low self could find a person along a thread of shadowy substance, the author arranged to be in an old house where he had a darkroom on various nights, without lights or any sign of his presence; the engineer was to come after dinner each evening and tell by his psychic ability whether the author was there, knocking if he was and going away if not. The test was made several times and it worked. Once the author tried to fool him by not answering the knock, and he refused to leave, calling out that he knew the author was inside."@en ;
    skos:note "A test with the structure of one: a target state the subject could not see, chosen by the experimenter, a response fixed in advance, repeated. What the report lacks is the count. Several times and it worked does not say on how many nights the author was there and how many not, nor whether the engineer ever went away from an occupied house or knocked at an empty one, and the one trial reported in detail is the one in which the author was inside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XVIII, case 28" .

#
# The rebuilt future
#

pmp:HunaPreparingThePrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The wish decided exactly and every contingency weighed before the prayer is made"@en, "Der Wunsch genau bestimmt und jede Möglichkeit erwogen, bevor das Gebet gesprochen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before the prayer to the High Self, the woman priest the author consulted in 1932 had him think hard and state exactly what he wanted to come to pass, then questioned him for an hour whether each part, if it happened so, would change his plans, so that no contingency was overlooked and each step weighed. The thought forms of the prayer had to be unmixed with doubts and stand out clear and sharp; any overlooked angle might bob up later and upset the plan."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaJumbledWishesJumbledFuture a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Changing wishes make a hit-and-miss future"@en, "Wechselnde Wünsche machen eine Zukunft aus Treffer und Fehlschlag"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the healer's experience most people send the High Self a continuous jumble of conflicting wishes, plans, fears and hopes and change their minds every day and hour. As the High Self makes our futures from our averaged thoughts, usually contacted during sleep, our futures become a hit-and-miss jumble of events and contrary events, accidents and good and bad luck. Only the person who decides what he wants and holds to it doggedly presents the thought forms from which the future can be built as desired."@en ;
    skos:note "Where an unfulfilled prayer goes: to the inconstancy of the one who prayed. Since every life has changing wishes, the condition is always met by anyone whose prayer was not answered."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaPreparingThePrayer ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmp:HunaWaterTumblerGazing a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A tumbler of water clouded with grated ginger, a silver dollar beneath it, gazed at under shaded eyes"@en, "Ein Wasserglas, mit geriebenem Ingwer getrübt, ein Silberdollar darunter, mit beschatteten Augen angeschaut"@de ;
    skos:definition "Instead of the black stone swished with water in a calabash, the healer filled a glass tumbler with water and grated half a teaspoon of fresh yellow ginger into it with her thumbnail, to cloud it and to ward off spirit influence of the poltergeist type; asked for a silver dollar as part of her fee and set it under the tumbler; shaded her eyes from the light and looked at the clouded surface, seeing images and getting messages by an inner voice for some seven or eight minutes. The symbols were read as favourable if she had learned by experience to know them as good: a door opening, a sheaf of wheat."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaTheScryerChecksTheClientsMind a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "She asked whether he had been thinking of the door and the wheat, to be sure she was not seeing his mind"@en, "Sie fragte, ob er an die Tür und den Weizen gedacht habe, um sicher zu sein, dass sie nicht seinen Geist sah"@de ;
    skos:definition "She asked what the door and the sheaf of wheat might mean to him or whether he had been thinking of them, wishing to be sure that she was not seeing them in his mind instead of from the High Self through the low self. When she saw his competitor and the competitor's office she checked with him whether she was seeing accurately."@en ;
    skos:note "A control the practitioner runs inside the procedure against the alternative her own system names, reading the client's mind. Its outcome is only ever reported one way: she was satisfied. Checking an image of the competitor's office with the client is a check of accuracy against his knowledge, which is the channel the first question was meant to exclude."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmp:HunaFastAndGiftToClearThePath a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Three days fasting until one o'clock without smoking, then a gift that hurts a little"@en, "Drei Tage Fasten bis ein Uhr ohne Rauchen, dann eine Gabe, die ein wenig weh tut"@de ;
    skos:definition "The door was not wide open and the path a little blocked; asked whether he had hurt anyone and whether he would be cheating at the price, the author said no, and the healer decided it was the little sin ideas from Sunday school that eat one inside. To clear the path he was to fast until one o'clock for three days and not smoke while fasting, and then give a gift to someone in need or a charity, large enough to hurt a little, almost more than he could afford, so that he would feel deep inside he had done enough to balance his little sins. He did so and gave to the Salvation Army."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaTheDoorWideOpen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The door seen again in the water, now wide open: the path unblocked"@en, "Die Tür wieder im Wasser gesehen, nun weit offen: der Weg frei"@de ;
    skos:definition "On his return in the evening she used the tumbler again and after a few minutes saw the door, this time wide open, and announced that his path was now unblocked."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign of success and the procedure's own check that the cleansing worked, seen by the scryer and read by her. It is counted with the signs and not with the conditions of failure. The one state the work reports short of it, a door not open all the way, was answered by more cleansing and not by declining the prayer."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaPrayerPresupposesTheClearedPath a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer is made only after the path is shown unblocked"@en, "Das Gebet wird erst gesprochen, wenn der Weg als frei gezeigt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The order of the case: the plan is made definite, the High Self is asked whether it can be made to materialise, the path is cleared by fasting and the gift until the door is wide open, and only then is the prayer made."@en ;
    skos:note "Set at prescribed order: the sequence is followed in the case and the healer gives the reason for the cleansing, but the passage does not say what would follow from praying with the path still a little blocked; she had already said the prayer could be answered."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HunaThriceSpokenPrayer ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HunaFastAndGiftToClearThePath ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmp:HunaThriceSpokenPrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer spoken three times in Hawaiian with gathered force"@en, "Das Gebet dreimal auf Hawaiisch mit gesammelter Kraft gesprochen"@de ;
    skos:definition "To make the prayer the healer rose and walked slowly back and forth breathing heavily, paused, said she would now pray to the god for him, and, looking as into a distance, spoke in Hawaiian slowly and with great force; she voiced the prayer once and repeated it twice, word for word as nearly as possible, then sat down and smoked to rest. The work explains that she had accumulated extra force and presented the prayer as thought forms on its flow, and that a priest's prayer for a client goes to the client's High Self too, all High Selves being linked."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaTheSaleAsForeseen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Next Tuesday at a quarter past two: the proposal on paper, ten minutes, and the sale at the price prayed for"@en, "Nächsten Dienstag um Viertel nach zwei: das Angebot auf Papier, zehn Minuten, und der Verkauf zum erbetenen Preis"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the prayer she saw in the water the new future the High Self had made in place of the old, and instructed him: write the proposition out in full, because the god told her the competitor was the kind who liked everything on paper and would otherwise say no from habit; next Tuesday at a quarter past two go to him; he will be sitting at his desk doing nothing; put the paper down, say you will be back in ten minutes, and come back. He will have read it and will buy. The author found the competitor idle at his desk at that hour, and on his return was told, I'll take you up, with a check for a hundred dollars to bind it; the price stipulated in the prayer, eight thousand dollars, was paid."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaPrayerYieldsTheRebuiltFuture a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer to the High Self replaces the feared future with the one asked for"@en, "Das Gebet an das Hohe Selbst ersetzt die gefürchtete Zukunft durch die erbetene"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaThriceSpokenPrayer ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaTheSaleAsForeseen ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    skos:note "The author is the client and vouches for each step; he says it is the one case he can give at first hand, and that it is chosen. He had gone to the competitor three times and paid an agent who failed; he went the fourth time with a written offer, at a time and with words prescribed to him, having followed every instruction to the letter. The case cannot separate the prayer from the written proposal, the healer's reading of the competitor, and the author's own changed approach, and the work does not try to."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

pmc:HunaEightBooksForetold a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "You will write eight books, and the last four will be easier and come faster"@en, "Du wirst acht Bücher schreiben, und die letzten vier werden leichter sein und schneller kommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before the author left for California the healer prayed that he be allowed to write and looked into the tumbler again: you will write eight books, that is as far ahead as the god shows me; you will have to be very patient; it will be a long time from the first book to the eighth; many things will happen and it will not be easy, although the last four books will be easier than the first four and come faster. The author writes that the glimpse dates from 1932 and that now, in 1947, the first four books are water under the bridge."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The author's published books, counted from the first after 1932: a number other than eight, or a fifth to eighth book that did not come faster than the first four. The prediction is dated, its count is fixed and its condition on the second half is stated, so the author's bibliography decides it; the work records four books by 1947 and stops there." ;
    skos:note "The only prediction in the chapters read that was printed before it was fulfilled, and so the only one whose outcome the work itself could not have chosen. Whether it came true is not examined here; the bibliography of Max Freedom Long was not read in this run, and the node carries no outcome. It is the place in this corpus where a protocol could be written with a case already fixed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XIX, case 29" .

#
# Weather
#

pmp:HunaWindsOfHiloPrayer a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer to the Winds of Hilo: the little winds into the calabash and the large ones let out, or the reverse"@en, "Das Gebet an die Winde von Hilo: die kleinen Winde in die Kalebasse und die großen hinaus, oder umgekehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A priest adept in weather control taught the surveyor N. S. Emerson the art: he introduced him several times to the god who controlled the weather and taught him to recite a ritual prayer to make the winds increase or decrease. The prayer, repeated aloud, calls on the Winds of Hilo and asks either that the little winds be put back in the wind calabash and the large ones let out, or the reverse. Nothing in it indicates the nature of its power; what counts, the work says, is what goes on in the low self."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXI, case 30" .

pmc:HunaWindControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "A good strong breeze within ten minutes of the prayer"@en, "Eine gute steife Brise binnen zehn Minuten nach dem Gebet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Emerson came to get results the same as his teacher and kept the contact all his life. Friends came to him to quiet the weather for passages between the islands, and on the annual kite day at the Kamehameha School for Boys he was invariably asked to make the prayer for the large winds to be let out so that the big kites could go up; the author has talked with a number of people who saw a good strong breeze blow up within ten minutes of the prayer."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXI, case 30" .

pmc:HunaWindPrayerYieldsWind a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "The prayer to the winds raises or quiets them"@en, "Das Gebet an die Winde hebt oder legt sie"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HunaWindsOfHiloPrayer ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HunaWindControl ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    skos:note "Ascribed by the author to Emerson on the word of witnesses he does not name; he says he never saw it himself. The selection of the reports is stated at pmc:HunaHeardOfNoFailure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXI, case 30" .

pmc:HunaHeardOfNoFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "If he ever failed the boys and their kites, I have heard nothing of the failure"@en, "Falls er die Jungen und ihre Drachen je im Stich ließ, habe ich nichts von dem Fehlschlag gehört"@de ;
    skos:definition "The closing sentence of the account of the kite days: I have talked with a number of people who have witnessed the blowing up of a good strong breeze within ten minutes after the making of the prayer, although I was never fortunate enough to see this myself. If he ever failed the boys and their kites, I have heard nothing of the failure."@en ;
    skos:note "The second statement of selection in the work, after pmc:HunaOmitsTheDreamsThatFailed, and the weaker form of it: not a rule of omission but a report that no failure reached the reporter. On a yearly occasion chosen for a windy season, with an interval of ten minutes and no stated base rate of a breeze rising in that time, the absence of a remembered failure is what one would expect whether the prayer did anything or not."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXI, case 30" .

pmc:HunaIntroductionToTheWeatherSpirit a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Weather control through a direct introduction by a priest who already knows the spirit"@en, "Wetterherrschaft durch eine unmittelbare Einführung seitens eines Priesters, der den Geist schon kennt"@de ;
    skos:definition "An appeal for weather control can be made through one's own High Self, who is supposed to pass it on to the High Self in charge of weather there; but there was a system of direct introduction: a priest who knew such a spirit, and so had a connecting thread to it, could introduce a student and help him set up a line of connection. The work sees in it the origin of ordination."@en ;
    skos:note "Set at prescribed order: the passage gives the introduction as a second route beside the appeal through one's own High Self, so it is not necessary, and it names no consequence of attempting the control without it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:HunaWindControl ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HunaWindsOfHiloPrayer ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXI, preliminary section and case 30" .

#
# The daily practice
#

pmp:HunaDailyRereading a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Read, reread, read and read and read"@en, "Lesen, wiederlesen, lesen und lesen und lesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For one who aspires to daily practice in contacting the High Self, the first help is the daily reading of writings that set before him the desirability and the complete possibility of success; the printed page is a physical stimulus to the low self, which will gradually fall into line if the middle self believes the teaching correct or even workable though illogical. Read each day the accounts in this report; dwell daily on the truth that there is no sin but hurting another, no hurt, no sin, and shout it from the housetops each hour for a year if need be."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXII" .

pmc:HunaTheGleamFadesUnread a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Without rereading, the gleam is confused in a week and forgotten in a month"@en, "Ohne Wiederlesen ist der Schimmer in einer Woche verworren und in einem Monat vergessen"@de ;
    skos:definition "At the first reading you will catch the gleam; a week later you will find it confused in your memory, because the new ideas have clashed with the old. If you reread, the gleam returns and stays longer; if you do not, the confusion will increase and in a month you will forget that you ever caught it. If we do not reread, the low self forgets and replaces the urge to practise with the old lazy urges."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution with stated terms, a week and a month, about the reader's belief in the book and not about any outcome of the practice. It is the work's own account of why a reader who stops believing has stopped, and it makes rereading the remedy for doubt."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HunaDailyRereading ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXII" .

pmc:HunaGroupsToRecordResults a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SecretScienceBehindMiracles ;
    rdfs:label "Groups that test who works best together, and bulletins giving the results of their experiments"@en, "Gruppen, die prüfen, wer am besten zusammenarbeitet, und Bulletins mit den Ergebnissen ihrer Versuche"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the practice of telepathy, on which prayer is said to rest, a partner is absolutely necessary, and in a group tests can be made to find out which people work best together, one concentrating on a symbol, a picture or a word and the others waiting for impressions. As individuals begin to get results in healing, fire-immunity and changing the future, a central organisation should clear findings from group to group, and bulletins giving the results of group and individual experiments would be in order."@en ;
    skos:note "The work closes by asking for the record it does not itself contain. A bulletin of results is the one instrument that would count the failures the cases leave out; the passage does not say whether failed experiments are to be reported, and the instruction for an individual who gets no result stands two chapters earlier (pmc:HunaNoResultsMeansAComplex)."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Huna ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter XXII" .

#
# The dispute over the two words
#

pmc:WhetherUnihipiliNamesASelfOfTheLiving a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether unihipili and aumakua name selves of a living person, or deities lodged in bones and ancestral gods"@en, "Ob unihipili und aumakua Selbste eines lebenden Menschen benennen oder in Knochen gebannte Gottheiten und Ahnengötter"@de ;
    skos:definition "The construction of Huna stands on reading unihipili as the subconscious and aumakua as the superconscious parental pair of each living person (pmc:HunaUnihipiliIsTheLowSelf, pmc:HunaAumakuaIsTheHighSelf). The dictionary of 1865 that the work itself quotes gives unihipili as the leg and arm bones of a person bound up together and worshipped in that condition, one name of the class of gods called akuanoho, aumakua being another, the departed spirits of deceased persons; and aumakua as a class of ancient gods considered able and trustworthy, the ancestors who died long ago and have become gods, and a house built for Kamehameha's bones that he might become such a god. Malo, in Emerson's translation, says that if after death a man's bones were set in position with an idol and his spirit came and made its residence with them, that was an akua noho, though specifically termed an unihipili or an aumakua; Emerson's note makes aumakua an ancestral deity whose worship passed from father to son, of whom a man might have several in case one was attending to another's prayer, and unihipili an artificial deity induced by a priest's incantation to reside in an image, a dead body or a bundle of bones, whose power ended when the offerings ceased and whose neglect might kill its keeper."@en ;
    skos:note "The two sides do not dispute a translation; they dispute the bearer. In both Hawaiian witnesses the words name beings made out of the dead, kept by a priest or a family and fed; in the work they name parts of the living person. The work knew the first reading, printed the dictionary entry on the page before overriding it, and gives as its reason that the missionaries had been given conflicting information. Malo is a Hawaiian of the old order, born about 1793, and Emerson a missionary's son, and neither can be read as untouched by the mission; but they agree with each other and with the dictionary on the point at issue, and the work does not cite a Hawaiian witness for its own reading."@en ;
    skos:note "What the dispute does and does not settle. It settles that the ascription of the three selves to the old priests has no support in the two Hawaiian sources read, which is what the registration of this tradition already said in prose and could not cite. It does not settle anything about the practices of the work: the fire walk, the death prayer and the cleansing before prayer are reported of Hawaiians of the author's own time, and whether they did what he says is a question about those reports and not about the two words."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:HunaUnihipiliIsTheLowSelf, pmc:HunaAumakuaIsTheHighSelf ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Max Freedom Long, The Secret Science Behind Miracles (Los Angeles: Kosmon Press, 1948), chapter I, the passage on the dictionary of 1865, and chapter III, the opening section", "Lorrin Andrews, A Dictionary of the Hawaiian Language (Honolulu: Henry M. Whitney, 1865), p. 124, s.v. unihipili, p. 33, s.v. aumakua, and p. 44, s.v. akua aumakua", "David Malo, Hawaiian Antiquities (Moolelo Hawaii), translated from the Hawaiian by N. B. Emerson (Honolulu: Hawaiian Gazette Co., 1903), chapter XXXII, section 2, p. 155, with Emerson's note 2, pp. 157-158" .
